# Canonical line-ending policy for this repository.
#
# Why this file exists: nothing here pins line endings, so a Windows checkout with
# `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites text files to CRLF in the working tree while git keeps
# LF in the index. A wheel built from that working tree then packages bytes that are
# not the committed bytes — the same commit produced three different wheel digests
# (8db713ca…, 98972a92…, f98b3839…) purely from where the build was run. A published
# checksum that a reviewer cannot reproduce from the commit is not evidence.
#
# `text=auto` leaves git's own binary detection in charge, so images and archives are
# never rewritten; `eol=lf` pins what a checkout writes for everything git considers
# text, on every platform, regardless of `core.autocrlf`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary payloads: state the ones this project actually carries, so a future change to
# git's detection rules cannot quietly reclassify them as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.whl binary
*.zip binary
*.pyc binary
*.sqlite3 binary
*.db binary

# Checksum manifests and build outputs are compared byte-for-byte by tooling on every
# platform; a CR byte in one makes `sha256sum -c` fail on Linux and macOS.
MANIFEST.sha256 text eol=lf
*.sha256 text eol=lf

# No shipped file is a Windows launcher. If one is ever added (.bat/.cmd/.ps1 need
# CRLF), declare it here explicitly with `eol=crlf` rather than relaxing this policy.
